  >
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>
      As part of my <strong>Networks & Telecommunications</strong> degree, I wanted to design and
      deploy my <strong>own VPN</strong>. Not to use a third-party service but to{' '}
      <strong>understand every layer</strong> that makes a secure tunnel.
    </p>
    <p>The goal was threefold:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Secure access</strong> to my home network from outside
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confidentiality</strong> of communications on public networks
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Technical mastery</strong>: PKI, TLS, NAT, iptables, routing
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <p>The system relies on a simple but complete flow:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        The <strong>smartphone</strong> initiates an OpenVPN connection on port{' '}
        <code>1194/UDP</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>Orange Livebox</strong> forwards traffic to the{' '}
        <strong>Kali Linux server</strong> (192.168.11.145)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenVPN</strong> authenticates the client via its X.509 certificate and establishes
        the <code>tun0</code> tunnel
      </li>
      <li>
        Traffic is routed via <strong>iptables MASQUERADE</strong> to the Internet
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Implementation steps</h2>

    <h3>1. Kali Linux preparation</h3>
    <p>
      First unexpected obstacle: <strong>the disk was full</strong> (19 GB used out of 19 GB). I had
      to clean system logs, APT caches and remove old kernels to free 4 GB.
    </p>

    <h3>2. PKI generation with Easy-RSA</h3>
    <p>
      The automatic installation script didn't generate the PKI correctly. So I manually created the
      Certificate Authority and certificates:
    </p>
    <pre><code>cd /etc/openvpn/easy-rsa
sudo ./easyrsa init-pki
sudo ./easyrsa build-ca nopass
sudo ./easyrsa gen-req server nopass
sudo ./easyrsa sign-req server server
sudo ./easyrsa gen-req client1 nopass
sudo ./easyrsa sign-req client client1
sudo ./easyrsa gen-dh</code></pre>

    <h3>3. OpenVPN configuration</h3>
    <p>
      The <code>/etc/openvpn/server.conf</code> file contains the server configuration. Key
      parameters:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>port 1194</code> and <code>proto udp</code>: standard OpenVPN port
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>dev tun</code>: tunnel interface type
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>server 10.8.0.0 255.255.255.0</code>: client IP range
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cipher AES-256-GCM</code>: symmetric encryption
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>tls-auth ta.key 0</code>: additional DoS protection
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. NAT & IP Forwarding</h3>
    <p>
      Without these two elements, VPN clients can connect but{' '}
      <strong>cannot access the Internet</strong>. It's the classic trap.
    </p>
    <pre><code># Enabling IP forwarding
echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
sudo sysctl -p

# iptables rules
sudo iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.8.0.0/24 -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -i tun0 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -o tun0 -j ACCEPT</code></pre>

    <h3>5. Livebox port forwarding</h3>
    <p>
      The trickiest step: the Orange box blocks incoming connections by default. Need to configure a{' '}
      <strong>NAT/PAT rule</strong>: port <code>1194/UDP</code> → <code>192.168.11.145</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Client .ovpn file</h3>
    <p>
      The <code>.ovpn</code> file is <strong>self-contained</strong>: it contains the configuration,
      CA certificate, client certificate and private key.
    </p>

    <h3>7. Tests & validation</h3>
    <p>
      Once connected from the smartphone, verification via <code>https://ifconfig.me</code>: the IP
      displayed is the Kali server one. The tunnel is operational.
    </p>

    <h2>Challenges encountered</h2>

    <h3>💾 Disk full at 100%</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: Kali Linux VM with 19 GB used out of 19 GB.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: cleaning logs, APT cache, removing old kernels. 4 GB freed.
    </p>

    <h3>🔐 Certificates not generated by script</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: the install script didn't create the PKI.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: manual generation with Easy-RSA (7 commands).
    </p>

    <h3>🔧 IP forwarding disabled</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: clients connected but couldn't browse.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: enabling <code>net.ipv4.ip_forward=1</code> in{' '}
      <code>/etc/sysctl.conf</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>🌐 NAT misconfigured</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: even with IP forwarding enabled, traffic wasn't going out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: adding the <code>MASQUERADE</code> rule in iptables.
    </p>

    <h3>🏠 Port inaccessible from outside</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: remote clients couldn't reach the server.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: configuring a NAT/PAT rule on the box (port 1194/UDP →
      192.168.11.145).
    </p>

    <h2>Result</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        ✅ Connection from an <strong>Android smartphone</strong> via OpenVPN Connect
      </li>
      <li>
        ✅ <strong>AES-256-GCM</strong> encryption
      </li>
      <li>
        ✅ <strong>X.509 certificate</strong> authentication
      </li>
      <li>
        ✅ <strong>Zero subscription</strong>, zero dependency on a third-party service
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Skills demonstrated</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Linux administration</strong>: Kali Linux, systemd, bash scripts
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Network security</strong>: VPN, TLS/SSL, PKI, X.509 certificates
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Network configuration</strong>: NAT, routing, IP forwarding, iptables, dynamic DNS
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Problem solving</strong>: diagnosis, logs, documentation
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Improvement paths</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Migration to WireGuard</strong>: faster, more modern
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Monitoring</strong>: Grafana + Prometheus
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ansible automation</strong>: replay deployment in 1 command
      </li>
    </ul>
